From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenMANET Pi 5 port <noreply@openmanet.org>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 21:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] bsp-bcm271x: gpsboard: make the WM1302 GPS init chip- and
 spi-path-agnostic

bsp-bcm271x is selected for the Pi 5 image (boards/ekh-bcm2712/target_diffconfig)
and its Makefile installs files/etc/init.d/* unconditionally, so gpsboard.init
runs on BCM2712. Two hardcoded BCM2711 assumptions make it a no-op there:

1. "gpioset -c gpiochip0" / "gpioget -c gpiochip0". On BCM2711 the 40-pin
   header GPIOs are gpiochip0, which is what the comment in the file asserts.
   On BCM2712 they belong to RP1, which sits behind PCIe and registers its
   gpio_chip with .base = -1 (pinctrl-rp1.c), while the SoC's two
   brcm,brcmstb-gpio controllers (&gio, &gio_aon) probe from the platform bus
   first. gpiochip0 on a Pi 5 is therefore a SoC controller whose line 25 is
   "BT_CTS" and whose line 12 is unnamed - so the driver would drive the wrong
   pins entirely. Resolve by gpio-line-name instead: 950-0870 gives both boards
   identical "GPIO2".."GPIO27" names for header pins, and -s/--strict aborts on
   an ambiguous name rather than guessing.

2. expected_path="platform/soc/fe204000.spi/spi_master/spi0/spi0.0" is the
   BCM2711 SPI0 address. On BCM2712 SPI0 is RP1's DesignWare SSI reached
   through PCIe, so the prefix differs completely and check_morse_section()
   takes its mismatch branch and "exit 0"s out of boot() before init_gps_gpio()
   ever runs - the GPS is silently never initialised. Match the suffix instead;
   what the check is actually asserting is that the morse radio is on SPI0
   chip-select 0, which is equally true on both boards.

Board-scoped to ekh-bcm2712: the Pi 4 product works with the current file and
CLAUDE.md prefers target-specific changes. Fold upstream once hardware-verified.

--- a/feeds/openmanet/boards/bsp-bcm271x/files/etc/init.d/gpsboard.init
+++ b/feeds/openmanet/boards/bsp-bcm271x/files/etc/init.d/gpsboard.init
@@ -29,15 +29,18 @@
     local gpio="$1"
     local value="$2"
     # Use gpioset with daemonize to persist GPIO state
-    # -c gpiochip0: specify the chip (GPIOs 0-57 are on gpiochip0)
+    # -s --by-name: resolve the header pin by its gpio-line-name across every
+    #   gpiochip, aborting if the name is not unique. "-c gpiochip0" is a
+    #   BCM2711 assumption; on BCM2712 the header pins live on RP1, which
+    #   probes from PCIe with .base = -1 and is not gpiochip0.
     # -C: set consumer name for identification in gpioinfo
     # -z: daemonize to keep GPIO state after exit (no & needed)
-    gpioset -c gpiochip0 -C "gps-wm1302" -z "${gpio}=${value}" 2>/dev/null
+    gpioset -s -C "gps-wm1302" -z --by-name "GPIO${gpio}=${value}" 2>/dev/null
 }
 
 get_gpio_val() {
     local gpio="$1"
-    gpioget -c gpiochip0 "${gpio}" 2>/dev/null || echo "?"
+    gpioget -s --by-name "GPIO${gpio}" 2>/dev/null || echo "?"
 }
 
 get_gpsd_device() {
@@ -52,7 +55,11 @@
 }
 
 check_morse_device() {
-    local expected_path="platform/soc/fe204000.spi/spi_master/spi0/spi0.0"
+    # A suffix, not a full path. On BCM2711 the radio is at
+    # platform/soc/fe204000.spi/spi_master/spi0/spi0.0; on BCM2712 SPI0 is
+    # RP1's DesignWare SSI behind PCIe, so the prefix differs entirely. The
+    # assertion that matters is "the morse radio is on SPI0 chip-select 0".
+    local expected_path="spi_master/spi0/spi0.0"
     local found=0
 
     # Iterate through all wifi-device sections
@@ -74,8 +81,8 @@
         if [ -z "$device_path" ]; then
             logger -t "$NAME" "morse wifi-device found but path does not exist"
             exit 0
-        elif [ "$device_path" != "$expected_path" ]; then
-            logger -t "$NAME" "morse wifi-device path does not match: got '$device_path', expected '$expected_path'"
+        elif [ "$device_path" = "${device_path%$expected_path}" ]; then
+            logger -t "$NAME" "morse wifi-device path does not match: got '$device_path', expected '*$expected_path'"
             exit 0
         fi
     fi
